WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.877
Store a linear attention state in BF16, and Qwen three point five,

00:00:04.877 --> 00:00:08.889
nine B, falls from eighty-seven point nine to seventy-two point one

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:10.348
on a math benchmark.

00:00:10.348 --> 00:00:10.713
Why?

00:00:13.165 --> 00:00:14.100
Hi, I'm Ludo!

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.840
LeapQuant stores this state in eight bits and keeps full precision accuracy.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:18.775
Here is how.

00:00:21.592 --> 00:00:22.256
Chapter one.

00:00:22.256 --> 00:00:23.917
A state read every token.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.500
Each Gated DeltaNet head keeps one matrix, a hundred and twenty-eight square.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.120
A new token brings a key, a correction and a decay.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.177
The update decays the matrix, then adds one rank-one write: the key,

00:00:40.177 --> 00:00:42.906
times what the memory got wrong about that key.

00:00:42.906 --> 00:00:44.421
That is the delta rule.

00:00:44.841 --> 00:00:47.516
Then the whole matrix goes back to memory.

00:00:47.516 --> 00:00:49.522
Every token, every head, every layer.

00:00:49.942 --> 00:00:53.618
On Qwen three point eight, twenty-seven B, that is a hundred and

00:00:53.618 --> 00:00:54.843
forty-four mebibytes per stream.

00:00:54.843 --> 00:00:57.293
Halve the bytes and you halve the bill.

00:00:59.108 --> 00:01:00.231
Here is the formula.

00:01:01.108 --> 00:01:02.056
First the decay.

00:01:02.056 --> 00:01:04.900
A slow head forgets a tiny fraction per token.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:06.379
Then one rank-one write.

00:01:06.379 --> 00:01:09.291
Each write is small next to the state it lands on.

00:01:11.092 --> 00:01:11.749
Chapter two.

00:01:11.749 --> 00:01:13.062
Why rounding piles up.

00:01:18.242 --> 00:01:21.325
Follow one entry of the state as it slowly decays.

00:01:21.325 --> 00:01:25.026
The faint lines are the only values a low-bit format can store.

00:01:25.446 --> 00:01:27.104
Round it after every token.

00:01:27.104 --> 00:01:31.082
Each change is smaller than half a level, so it rounds straight

00:01:31.082 --> 00:01:33.403
back, and the stored value never moves.

00:01:33.403 --> 00:01:37.713
In BF16, a decay of zero point nine nine nine is erased completely.

00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:42.536
LeapQuant holds the stored state fixed, buffers sixteen tokens of updates in

00:01:42.536 --> 00:01:44.738
higher precision, and applies them exactly.

00:01:44.738 --> 00:01:47.306
It rounds once, at the window's end.

00:01:47.726 --> 00:01:51.675
A few roundings instead of one per token, and nothing small is thrown away.

00:01:53.675 --> 00:01:59.138
The paper measured this on Qwen three point five over sixty-four thousand tokens.

00:01:59.138 --> 00:02:02.919
Rounded every token, INT8 and BF16 both drift away.

00:02:03.339 --> 00:02:06.794
Rounded once per window, the error stays near the floor, and LeapQuant's

00:02:06.794 --> 00:02:08.234
line is lowest of all.

00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:10.404
Chapter three.

00:02:10.404 --> 00:02:12.467
Lift the outliers, smooth the rest.

00:02:16.158 --> 00:02:17.744
Here is one value channel.

00:02:17.744 --> 00:02:21.549
Its scale is set by its largest entry, so two outlier rows

00:02:21.549 --> 00:02:24.403
leave ordinary entries on a grid far too coarse.

00:02:24.823 --> 00:02:26.903
LeapQuant subtracts the strongest rank-one pieces.

00:02:26.903 --> 00:02:31.062
Each has the shape of a token's write, so it rides in

00:02:31.062 --> 00:02:33.142
the window buffer, kept in FP16.

00:02:33.562 --> 00:02:36.919
Smoothing then divides each key row by the square root of its

00:02:36.919 --> 00:02:39.157
mean size, and multiplies back when it reads.

00:02:39.577 --> 00:02:43.470
On one real Qwen three point five head, the peak falls from

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:47.687
a hundred and seventy-six times the median, to forty-seven, to five point three.

00:02:49.467 --> 00:02:51.526
The authors animate the same idea.

00:02:51.526 --> 00:02:55.644
Outlier rows and columns lift out as Compensator Tokens, and the rest

00:02:55.644 --> 00:02:58.732
is rescaled row by row until it is flat.

00:03:00.633 --> 00:03:04.997
The paper's picture of a window: residual, plus Compensator Tokens, plus buffered

00:03:04.997 --> 00:03:07.906
real tokens, add up to the full state.

00:03:08.326 --> 00:03:11.729
The compensators sit in the same list as real tokens, so the

00:03:11.729 --> 00:03:13.147
kernel needs no second path.

00:03:14.842 --> 00:03:19.453
A window starts from the stored state: an INT8 residual, its scales

00:03:19.453 --> 00:03:20.989
and four FP16 tokens.

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:24.447
One point one nine bytes per value, against four.

00:03:24.867 --> 00:03:28.475
Each decode step reads that state and the buffer, and appends its

00:03:28.475 --> 00:03:29.678
own key and correction.

00:03:29.678 --> 00:03:30.881
It writes no state.

00:03:31.301 --> 00:03:34.536
After sixteen tokens, the kernel rebuilds the full state.

00:03:34.956 --> 00:03:39.099
It fits four new Compensator Tokens, subtracts them, and smooths the rows.

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:41.976
Then it rounds once, and the next window begins.

00:03:43.883 --> 00:03:44.432
Chapter four.

00:03:44.432 --> 00:03:45.531
Does it hold up?

00:03:50.633 --> 00:03:51.864
The paper's ablation.

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:55.147
INT8 rounded every token scores seven point one.

00:03:55.147 --> 00:04:00.070
The window lifts it to eighty-two point four, compensators to eighty-six point

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:03.763
six, and smoothing to full precision's eighty-seven point nine.

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:07.291
Kernel throughput for one layer.

00:04:07.291 --> 00:04:11.468
At batch five hundred and twelve, Gated DeltaNet runs two point six

00:04:11.468 --> 00:04:13.557
eight times faster on a B200.

00:04:13.977 --> 00:04:18.499
And four point two five times on an RTX fifty ninety, the best cell.

00:04:18.499 --> 00:04:22.051
End to end, the paper averages one point five seven times.

00:04:23.525 --> 00:04:25.806
The open code is narrower than the paper.

00:04:26.226 --> 00:04:27.289
It is eight-bit only.

00:04:29.157 --> 00:04:32.926
It writes the low-bit state inside the full precision slot, so it

00:04:32.926 --> 00:04:34.183
saves traffic, not memory.

00:04:34.603 --> 00:04:39.113
It refuses prefix caching, which is where the paper measured its memory savings.

00:04:39.533 --> 00:04:43.120
And its kernels use copy engines that arrived with Hopper, so an

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:44.614
RTX thirty ninety is out.

00:04:45.808 --> 00:04:49.145
A low-bit recurrent state works, if you round it rarely, and

00:04:49.145 --> 00:04:50.359
make each rounding small.

00:04:52.817 --> 00:04:55.225
Round rarely, lift the outliers, smooth the rest.

00:04:55.225 --> 00:04:56.429
See you next time!

00:04:57.417 --> 00:04:59.430
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:00.005
I'm Ludo.

00:05:00.005 --> 00:05:00.292
Bye!
